# Writing a {{stinger.name}} server

A server is the *provider* of the {{stinger.name}} interface. It {{ server_roles[:-1]|join(', ') }}{% if server_roles|length > 2 %},{% endif %}{% if server_roles|length > 1 %} and {% endif %}{{ server_roles[-1] }}.
Everything it does goes through [`{{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}`](crate::server::{{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}), which needs the `server` feature.

More than one server instance may be on a broker at the same time, so each one is given
an **instance id** at construction. That id goes into every topic the server publishes
on, and it is what a client picks between during discovery.

## Wiring one up

There are four steps, in this order:

1. Create and start an MQTT client — any type that implements `Mqtt5PubSub`.
{%- if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}
2. Implement [`{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers`](crate::server::{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers) — one `async fn` per method.
{%- else %}
2. Decide on an instance id for this server.
{%- endif %}
3. Construct the server{% if stinger.properties|length > 0 %}, handing it the initial value of every property{% endif %}.
4. Spawn [`run_loop`](crate::server::{{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}::run_loop) and keep the handle alive.

Until `run_loop` is running, the server is subscribed but not processing: incoming
messages queue up rather than being dispatched.

```ignore
use std::sync::Arc;
use tokio::sync::Mutex;
{%- if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}
use {{stinger.rust.package_name}}::server::{{'{'}}{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers, {{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}};
{%- else %}
use {{stinger.rust.package_name}}::server::{{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}};
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.properties|length > 0 %}
use {{stinger.rust.package_name}}::property::{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}InitialPropertyValues;
{%- endif %}

// 1. Your MQTT client, already connected.
let connection = /* ... */;
{% if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}
// 2. Your implementation of the interface's methods.
let handlers: Arc<Mutex<Box<dyn {{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers<_>>>> =
    Arc::new(Mutex::new(Box::new(MyHandlers::new())));
{% endif %}
// 3. Construct the server.
let mut server = {{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}::new(
    connection,
{%- if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}
    handlers.clone(),
{%- endif %}
    "my-instance-1".to_string(),
{%- if stinger.properties|length > 0 %}
    initial_property_values,
{%- endif %}
{%- for topic_param in config.topics.params %}
    {{topic_param|snake_case}},   // topic parameter
{%- endfor %}
).await;

// 4. Keep the message loop running for as long as the server should serve.
let mut server_for_loop = server.clone();
tokio::spawn(async move { server_for_loop.run_loop().await });
```

Constructing the server also publishes the interface's **discovery message**, which is
how clients find this instance. It advertises the instance id, the interface version,
{%- if stinger.methods|length > 0 or stinger.commands|length > 0 %} the version of each method and command,{% endif %} and the current value of every property. Call
[`shutdown`](crate::server::{{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}::shutdown) to retract that
announcement when the process is going away on purpose.
{%- if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}

## Methods

Every method in the interface is one required `async fn` on the
[`{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers`](crate::server::{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers)
trait, so the compiler will not let you forget one. Each returns
`Result<T, MethodReturnCode>`: returning `Err` sends that return code back to the calling
client instead of a value.

The trait's `initialize` is called once with a clone of the server, which is how a
handler gets the ability to{% if stinger.signals|length > 0 %} emit signals or{% endif %} change properties from inside a method call.

```ignore
#[async_trait]
impl {{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}MethodHandlers<MyMqttClient> for MyHandlers {
    async fn initialize(&mut self, server: {{stinger.rust.server_struct_name}}<MyMqttClient>) -> Result<(), MethodReturnCode> {
        self.server = Some(server);
        Ok(())
    }
{% for method_name, method in stinger.methods.items() %}
    async fn handle_{{method_name|snake_case}}(&self{% for arg in method.arg_list %}, {{arg.name|snake_case}}: {{arg.rust.type}}{% endfor %}) -> Result<{{method.rust.return_value_type}}, MethodReturnCode> {
        todo!("{{method_name}}")
    }
{% endfor %}
    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn std::any::Any { self }
}
```

The methods of this interface are:
{% for method_name, method in stinger.methods.items() %}
- **`{{method_name}}`** → `handle_{{method_name|snake_case}}`{% if method.arg_list|length > 0 %} taking {% for arg in method.arg_list %}`{{arg.name|snake_case}}: {{arg.rust.type}}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% else %} taking no arguments{% endif %}, returning `{{method.rust.return_value_type}}`.
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.signals|length > 0 %}

## Signals

Signals go server → client with no reply, so emitting one cannot fail in the sense of
being rejected — it can only fail to reach the broker. Each signal has two forms:

- `emit_<signal>(args…).await` returns a future you can await to learn that the publish
  was accepted by the broker.
- `emit_<signal>_nowait(args…)` publishes and returns immediately.

Either way, a signal is only delivered to clients that are subscribed *at the time it is
sent*; there is no replay for a client that connects later.

```ignore
{%- for sig_name, sig in stinger.signals.items() %}
server.emit_{{sig_name|snake_case}}({% for arg in sig.arg_list %}{{arg.name|snake_case}}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}).await;
{%- endfor %}
```

The signals of this interface are:
{% for sig_name, sig in stinger.signals.items() %}
- **`{{sig_name}}`** — `emit_{{sig_name|snake_case}}({% for arg in sig.arg_list %}{{arg.name|snake_case}}: {{arg.rust.type}}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %})`
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.commands|length > 0 %}

## Commands

Commands arrive client → server with no reply. On the server they are handed out as
[`tokio::sync::broadcast`] receivers, the same way the *client* receives signals, and for
the same reason: any number of places in your program may consume the same command, and
consuming it is entirely optional. A command that arrives with nothing subscribed is
logged and dropped.

Because a broadcast channel does not replay, **subscribe before the command arrives** —
in practice, right after constructing the server and before spawning `run_loop`.

There is no response channel, so a command that fails to deserialize or fails schema
validation is logged and dropped. The server never publishes an error back, and it never
learns which client sent the command.

```ignore
{%- for cmd_name, cmd in stinger.commands.items() %}
let mut {{cmd_name|snake_case}}_rx = server.get_{{cmd_name|snake_case}}_receiver();
tokio::spawn(async move {
    while let Ok(payload) = {{cmd_name|snake_case}}_rx.recv().await {
        // handle {{cmd_name}}
    }
});
{% endfor -%}
```

The commands of this interface are:
{% for cmd_name, cmd in stinger.commands.items() %}
- **`{{cmd_name}}`** — `get_{{cmd_name|snake_case}}_receiver() -> broadcast::Receiver<{{cmd.payload.rust.channel_type}}>`
{%- endfor %}

The payload type follows the number of arguments: `()` for none, the argument's own type
for exactly one, and a generated `<Name>CommandPayload` struct for several.
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.properties|length > 0 %}

## Properties

The server owns every property value. Clients hold a cached copy and may *request* a
change; the server decides whether to apply it and publishes the value that actually
took effect{%if config.properties.version_tracking%}, along with a monotonically increasing version number{%endif%}.

Three accessors per property:

- `watch_<property>()` — a `tokio::sync::watch` receiver, for observing changes
  (including ones a client requested).
- `set_<property>(value)` — set the value and publish it to all clients.
- `get_<property>_handle()` — the underlying lock, when you need to read-modify-write
  under one commit rather than clobbering a concurrent change.

Property values are published retained, so a client that subscribes later still gets the
current value — properties, unlike signals, do not lose their state.  Clients subscribe to
a property only when their application asks for it, so a property nobody reads costs
nothing on the wire beyond that one retained message.

The properties of this interface are:
{% for prop_name, prop in stinger.properties.items() %}
- **`{{prop_name}}`**: `{{prop.rust.type}}`{% if prop.read_only %} — read-only, so clients can watch it but cannot request changes{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.uses_schemas() %}

## Schema validation

Some arguments in this interface carry JSON Schema constraints. The server checks them at
every boundary — method request and response, property update and publish, and signal
emit — and refuses to send or accept non-conforming data, failing with the appropriate
`MethodReturnCode` rather than putting bad data on the wire. Payload types expose
`validate_schema()` if you want to check a value yourself first.
{%- endif %}

## Errors and diagnostics

[`MethodReturnCode`](crate::payloads::MethodReturnCode) is the shared error type on the
wire, covering client and server faults, serialization failures, timeouts, and
`NotImplemented`. Anything the server logs goes through the `tracing` crate, so install a
subscriber to see it. With the `metrics` feature, `get_metrics()` exposes counters for
messages handled and errors seen.
